export const prerender = true;

import Tutorial from "../../layouts/Tutorial.astro";
---

<Tutorial
  title="Read an audit report - Laravel SEO Tutorials"
  description="A tab-by-tab guide to the Laravel SEO audit report: Overview, SEO, Performance, Security, Lighthouse, Black SEO and the Site Crawler."
  heading="Read an audit report"
  intro="Each audit report is organised into tabs so you can go from the headline score to the exact check that failed. This guide walks through every tab using a real report."
>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/images/app/report-detail.png"
      alt="The audit report for drupalcompany.com: score 91 out of 100, one high issue, one medium, four low, 27 tests passed"
      width="1280"
      height="720"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>A real report: drupalcompany.com scoring 91/100, 23 hours after the crawl.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    The landing tab. You get the score ring, the page's title and meta description as Google would see them, and the
    issue summary: how many <strong>high</strong>, <strong>medium</strong> and <strong>low</strong> issues were found
    and how many tests passed. Underneath sit the raw page stats: load time, page weight, resource count and whether
    the connection is secure.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the report above: 1 high issue, 1 medium, 4 low, 27 tests passed, a 0.43 second load time and a 9.15 kB page.
    That is what a healthy site looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>SEO</h2>
  <p>
    The on-page checks: title and meta description length, headings structure, image alt attributes, links,
    canonicals, Open Graph tags and indexability. The counter on the tab tells you how many issues live there before
    you open it.
  </p>

  <h2>Performance</h2>
  <p>
    What slows the page down: response time, page size, number of requests, compression and caching headers. Fix
    these first when the score drops; they are usually the cheapest wins.
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    HTTPS configuration and the response headers that protect visitors: HSTS, content-type options, frame
    protection and friends. A missing header shows up here with the exact name to add.
  </p>

  <h2>Lighthouse</h2>
  <p>
    A full Google Lighthouse audit of the same URL, inside the report, so you do not have to run it separately.
    You can also run Lighthouse as a <a href="/tutorials/free-tools">free standalone tool</a> without an account.
  </p>

  <h2>Black SEO</h2>
  <p>
    Checks for patterns search engines penalise: hidden text, keyword stuffing, sneaky redirects and similar
    black-hat signals. Useful when you inherit a site and want to know what the previous SEO actually did.
  </p>

  <h2>Site Crawler</h2>
  <p>
    Crawls the site beyond the single page you scanned, following links within the limits you set, and reports
    broken links and dead ends. Maximum pages and crawl depth are configurable, and you can import a sitemap to
    seed the crawl.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do with a bad score</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Fix the <strong>high</strong> issues first; they move the score the most.</li>
    <li>Re-run the audit on the same URL after deploying the fix.</li>
    <li>Compare the new report with the old one in your reports list to confirm the issue count went down.</li>
  </ol>
</Tutorial>
